Vaginitis
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Abnormal vaginal discharge, Vulvovaginal pruritus/burning, Vaginal mucosal erythema

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-049
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe burning, intense vulvar swelling, and profuse frothy discharge following recent unprotected intercourse.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of agonizing vulvovaginal burning, massive labial edema, and a profuse, malodorous discharge, causing severe distress and fear of a severe sexually transmitted infection. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively diagnose the etiology (ruling out Pelvic Inflammatory Disease), provide immediate targeted antimicrobial therapy, and de-escalate acute psychological distress before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My entire vaginal area is swollen, burning intensely, and I have this horrible, smelly, yellowish discharge that started yesterday.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced aggressively 24 hours ago. The patient reports a highly noticeable change in the volume, color, and odor of her vaginal discharge, describing it as profuse, frothy, and yellow-green. She complains of intense vulvovaginal pruritus and a severe burning sensation. She describes extreme external dysuria as urine flows over the inflamed labial tissue, and severe dyspareunia secondary to tissue inflammation. She reports a malodorous scent. She admits to a new sexual partner one week ago.
Functional Status: Ambulatory but walking with a wide-based gait due to severe labial swelling and chafing.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute anxiety regarding STI transmission and severe physical discomfort.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious, flushed, and in visible discomfort when seated.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft, completely non-tender, with no suprapubic or lower quadrant guarding. Visual inspection of the external genitalia reveals marked erythema and severe edema of the vulva and labia minora. Speculum examination (performed with care due to friability) reveals severe vaginal mucosal erythema. A profuse, frothy, yellow-green discharge is pooled in the vaginal vault. The cervix exhibits punctate hemorrhages (classic "strawberry cervix"). Vaginal pH testing is elevated at 6.0. Bimanual exam reveals no cervical motion tenderness and no adnexal tenderness.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Trichomonas Vaginitis - Status: Severe acute flare
Differentials:

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Ruled out by the complete absence of lower abdominal pain, lack of fever, and the normal bimanual exam with no cervical motion or adnexal tenderness.

Bacterial Vaginosis - Ruled out by the presence of severe mucosal inflammation (erythema/edema) and the frothy, yellow-green nature of the discharge, though the pH is elevated in both.

Vulvovaginal Candidiasis - Ruled out by the frothy, yellow-green discharge (not thick and curdy), the elevated pH (not normal), and the strawberry cervix appearance.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, acute vaginitis characterized by profuse frothy yellow-green discharge, intense burning, and a classic "strawberry cervix." The objective clinical evidence, combined with an elevated pH and severe mucosal inflammation, strongly points to Trichomoniasis rather than bacterial vaginosis or a yeast infection. Crucially, the absence of abdominal and cervical motion tenderness safely rules out an ascending Pelvic Inflammatory Disease. The severity of the burning pain, massive labial swelling, and acute psychological distress mandated urgent after-hours evaluation to provide immediate, targeted single-dose antimicrobial therapy and reassurance, preventing an unnecessary emergency department visit for an STI screen.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Metronidazole 2g stat (single massive dose) for definitive treatment of Trichomonas. Administered a topical lidocaine 2% gel to the external vulva to provide immediate relief from the severe burning and dysuria. Collected high vaginal and endocervical swabs for full STI screening (NAAT for Chlamydia/Gonorrhea, Trichomonas culture) to be processed tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change: To completely prevent the agonizing stinging when passing urine, pour a large jug of lukewarm water directly over the vulva while you void; experiencing the total absence of the burning sensation will reinforce this protective hygienic habit. To prevent ping-pong reinfection, absolutely abstain from any sexual contact until both you and your partner have completed treatment and your symptoms have entirely resolved; understanding the high transmission rate will ensure strict compliance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP or sexual health clinic review in one week to review full STI swab results and ensure symptom resolution.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or local urgent care if you develop a high fever, if you begin experiencing severe, deep lower abdominal or pelvic pain, or if you develop intractable vomiting.

